{"answer":"For every real x with x≠2,\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}=x+2.\n\\]\nThe original quotient has domain \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{2\\}\\). The polynomial \\(x+2\\) is defined at every real number, but the original quotient is undefined at \\(x=2\\); hence they agree exactly on \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{2\\}\\) and are not equivalent as total real-valued functions.","approaches":[{"description":"Factor the numerator and cancel the common factor only after retaining the nonzero-denominator condition.","id":"factor-domain","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This yields the simplification on precisely the domain of the original quotient."},{"description":"Treat the cancelled form as assigning a value to the original quotient at x=2.","id":"global-cancel","outcome":"rejected","reason":"The original denominator is zero at x=2, while x+2=4 there."}],"change_log":["Retained the domain restriction explicitly throughout cancellation.","Made the excluded-case comparison explicit: the factorization remains valid at x=2, but cancellation is used only for x≠2.","No audit objections remain; the failed tool receipts were not used as evidence."],"claims":[{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Direct expansion in factor-identity.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"factorization","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x, x²−4=(x−2)(x+2).","step_ids":["factor-identity"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Domain analysis in denominator-condition and original-domain-step.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"original-domain-claim","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Over the reals, the domain of (x²−4)/(x−2) is ℝ\\{2}.","step_ids":["denominator-condition","original-domain-step"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Substitution of the factorization and cancellation of the explicitly nonzero factor in restricted-cancellation.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["factorization","original-domain-claim"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"restricted-simplification","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"For every real x∈ℝ\\{2}, (x²−4)/(x−2)=x+2.","step_ids":["restricted-cancellation"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The common-domain equality and excluded-input comparison in agreement-domain.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["original-domain-claim","restricted-simplification"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"not-global-equivalence","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The quotient and x+2 agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}, but are not equivalent as total real-valued functions.","step_ids":["compare-at-two","agreement-domain"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"factor-identity","justification":"Expanding the right-hand side gives x²+2x−2x−4=x²−4. This identity includes x=2 and uses no division.","statement":"For every real x, establish x²−4=(x−2)(x+2)."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"denominator-condition","justification":"A quotient is defined only when its denominator is nonzero, and x−2=0 if and only if x=2.","statement":"The denominator x−2 is nonzero exactly when x≠2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["denominator-condition"],"id":"original-domain-step","justification":"For x≠2 the denominator is nonzero and the polynomial numerator is defined; at x=2 the denominator is zero.","statement":"The original quotient has domain ℝ\\{2}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["factor-identity","denominator-condition"],"id":"restricted-cancellation","justification":"If x≠2, then x−2 is nonzero. Substituting factor-identity gives ((x−2)(x+2))/(x−2), and cancelling this nonzero factor gives x+2.","statement":"For each real x≠2, simplify the quotient to x+2."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["original-domain-step"],"id":"compare-at-two","justification":"At x=2, x+2=4. By original-domain-step, the original quotient is undefined at x=2.","statement":"At the excluded input x=2, the polynomial has value 4 whereas the quotient has no value."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["restricted-cancellation","compare-at-two"],"id":"agreement-domain","justification":"Restricted-cancellation proves equality for every x≠2; compare-at-two shows that equality cannot be asserted at x=2 as an equality of total real-valued functions.","statement":"The two expressions agree exactly on ℝ\\{2}, not at every real input."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
